{"What counts as an unfair act under UDAAP?": [[0, 0.7809140682220459], [3, 0.7588494420051575], [8, 0.740759015083313], [50, 0.7385772466659546], [2, 0.7357079982757568], [47, 0.7350970506668091], [33, 0.7271654605865479], [41, 0.7256709933280945], [39, 0.7183316946029663], [51, 0.7169408798217773]], "What specific criteria determine whether a practice is unfair under the UDAAP standards": [[2, 0.7815553545951843], [8, 0.7813640832901001], [3, 0.7730026245117188], [0, 0.7622562646865845], [5, 0.7548321485519409], [9, 0.7527248859405518], [47, 0.7500924468040466], [50, 0.7498027086257935], [41, 0.7401080131530762], [10, 0.7336598634719849]], "What constitutes substantial injury and lack of reasonable consumer avoidability for an unfair act": [[5, 0.8694617748260498], [3, 0.8373699188232422], [4, 0.8038716316223145], [7, 0.7981775999069214], [9, 0.7974429130554199], [8, 0.7792885899543762], [11, 0.7720286250114441], [2, 0.7714321613311768], [14, 0.7319269180297852], [10, 0.7233647108078003]], "Legal definition and elements required to establish an unfair practice in regulatory examinations": [[2, 0.7020688652992249], [9, 0.6875102519989014], [41, 0.6832743287086487], [34, 0.6824461817741394], [5, 0.6724517345428467], [3, 0.6672890186309814], [10, 0.6648464202880859], [7, 0.660845935344696], [8, 0.6519310474395752], [50, 0.6436103582382202]], "How does UDAAP define an abusive practice?": [[0, 0.7792354822158813], [33, 0.7713912725448608], [2, 0.7442753314971924], [41, 0.7335160970687866], [47, 0.7298806309700012], [50, 0.7270371913909912], [3, 0.722679615020752], [34, 0.714295506477356], [51, 0.7076441049575806], [8, 0.7071356773376465]], "What are the statutory criteria for establishing an abusive act or practice under the CFPB examination manual?": [[2, 0.7881506681442261], [0, 0.7650733590126038], [33, 0.7622820734977722], [34, 0.7578263282775879], [41, 0.7550140619277954], [3, 0.7280403971672058], [50, 0.7182469367980957], [17, 0.7169608473777771], [21, 0.7148122787475586], [8, 0.7147142887115479]], "How does the regulatory framework determine when a financial practice meets the threshold of being abusive?": [[34, 0.745476245880127], [2, 0.7408087849617004], [41, 0.7234410047531128], [33, 0.7151579856872559], [0, 0.6946552991867065], [5, 0.6864447593688965], [50, 0.6772897839546204], [3, 0.6742945909500122], [8, 0.6709517240524292], [47, 0.669520378112793]], "What specific elements must be present to classify conduct as abusive according to UDAAP standards?": [[0, 0.7563906908035278], [33, 0.7550944089889526], [34, 0.7389415502548218], [41, 0.7344120740890503], [50, 0.7340683937072754], [47, 0.729438066482544], [2, 0.7290847897529602], [44, 0.7187588810920715], [51, 0.7154560685157776], [3, 0.7088327407836914]], "Can we hide fees in the fine print?": [[53, 0.6679425239562988], [27, 0.6495102643966675], [28, 0.6394280791282654], [50, 0.6348836421966553], [19, 0.6343412399291992], [56, 0.6311914920806885], [21, 0.6288571953773499], [24, 0.6285366415977478], [52, 0.6170528531074524], [60, 0.6151343584060669]], "Is it considered a deceptive practice to conceal material fee disclosures within fine print?": [[19, 0.7500356435775757], [53, 0.7297154664993286], [20, 0.7242733240127563], [21, 0.7220021486282349], [24, 0.7126266956329346], [28, 0.7057149410247803], [26, 0.6940796375274658], [18, 0.6938786506652832], [22, 0.6876074075698853], [27, 0.6867965459823608]], "What are the regulatory standards regarding the prominent display of fees versus burying them in disclosures?": [[53, 0.7035263776779175], [24, 0.6595636606216431], [52, 0.6566839814186096], [21, 0.6564772129058838], [27, 0.6523830890655518], [28, 0.6474878787994385], [20, 0.6425447463989258], [19, 0.6392509937286377], [29, 0.6356421709060669], [32, 0.6351619958877563]], "Does obscuring fee information in fine print constitute a deceptive omission under UDAAP?": [[21, 0.7454328536987305], [29, 0.734015166759491], [27, 0.7181763648986816], [19, 0.7180424928665161], [25, 0.7172825932502747], [50, 0.715717613697052], [28, 0.7147294282913208], [17, 0.7141506671905518], [24, 0.7100780606269836], [52, 0.7066598534584045]], "Is it okay to pressure customers into signing up quickly?": [[22, 0.6016135811805725], [52, 0.5957306623458862], [28, 0.5955713987350464], [27, 0.5923475027084351], [20, 0.5906018614768982], [47, 0.5847159624099731], [56, 0.5845273733139038], [19, 0.5842376351356506], [9, 0.5826684236526489], [60, 0.5825495719909668]], "Do high-pressure sales tactics and time-sensitive demands violate federal prohibitions against unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts or practices?": [[0, 0.6868208050727844], [47, 0.6765883564949036], [41, 0.6752569675445557], [34, 0.6744749546051025], [33, 0.6725515127182007], [2, 0.6724451780319214], [57, 0.6644875407218933], [50, 0.6638398170471191], [20, 0.6632207632064819], [5, 0.6625865697860718]], "Are coercive sales methods that rush consumers into making hurried purchasing decisions considered deceptive under regulatory standards?": [[19, 0.712786078453064], [20, 0.7032909393310547], [26, 0.7010635137557983], [22, 0.6967096328735352], [18, 0.6817770004272461], [21, 0.6763365268707275], [34, 0.6738030910491943], [23, 0.6704921126365662], [2, 0.6699631214141846], [40, 0.6655479669570923]], "What are the regulatory risks associated with inducing consumers to execute agreements without adequate time to review material information?": [[50, 0.6740278005599976], [44, 0.6637814044952393], [33, 0.6624722480773926], [32, 0.6586711406707764], [21, 0.6584224104881287], [2, 0.6566325426101685], [0, 0.6559205055236816], [24, 0.6554272174835205], [42, 0.6546715497970581], [29, 0.6531167030334473]], "What makes a disclosure misleading to a reasonable consumer?": [[22, 0.7822851538658142], [28, 0.7712351083755493], [19, 0.7704499959945679], [21, 0.7645696401596069], [18, 0.7605186104774475], [20, 0.7493273019790649], [23, 0.7472862601280212], [53, 0.7369036674499512], [24, 0.7140716314315796], [31, 0.7120290994644165]], "What standards determine if a disclosure misleads a reasonable consumer under deceptive practices examination criteria": [[21, 0.8099538087844849], [22, 0.8019992113113403], [20, 0.7833374738693237], [19, 0.7796683311462402], [18, 0.7706228494644165], [23, 0.7485565543174744], [28, 0.7400238513946533], [2, 0.7325354814529419], [40, 0.7310972213745117], [53, 0.7297554016113281]], "How do regulators evaluate whether consumer information or disclosures create a material misunderstanding": [[21, 0.7328320741653442], [18, 0.7225459218025208], [24, 0.7204537987709045], [23, 0.7158695459365845], [31, 0.7131586074829102], [28, 0.71019446849823], [53, 0.7090135812759399], [19, 0.708412766456604], [22, 0.7077706456184387], [36, 0.7013760805130005]], "What constitutes a misleading disclosure in the context of consumer perception and decision-making": [[19, 0.7444658875465393], [28, 0.7387832999229431], [22, 0.7286583185195923], [21, 0.7283023595809937], [20, 0.7241119146347046], [18, 0.7210108637809753], [53, 0.7195533514022827], [23, 0.7028935551643372], [24, 0.7020041942596436], [26, 0.6807445883750916]], "What is the minimum credit score for a mortgage?": [[30, 0.5411309599876404], [56, 0.5409952998161316], [31, 0.5396179556846619], [32, 0.5320438742637634], [11, 0.5302119255065918], [12, 0.5292928814888], [60, 0.5273752808570862], [13, 0.5233125686645508], [55, 0.5219619274139404], [54, 0.5144683122634888]], "What is the minimum credit score requirement to qualify for a mortgage loan under regulatory standards": [[32, 0.5900434255599976], [56, 0.5868723392486572], [55, 0.5840497016906738], [60, 0.573698878288269], [45, 0.5723751187324524], [1, 0.5633916854858398], [0, 0.5628028512001038], [31, 0.5626015067100525], [33, 0.5622494220733643], [54, 0.5621733665466309]], "What credit score threshold constitutes a qualifying minimum for mortgage origination": [[56, 0.5835049152374268], [55, 0.569946825504303], [60, 0.5689302086830139], [54, 0.5610700845718384], [31, 0.5586906671524048], [30, 0.5574474334716797], [32, 0.5572099089622498], [48, 0.5539202690124512], [45, 0.5507001876831055], [20, 0.5488904118537903]], "What are the established credit score minimums mandated for residential mortgage underwriting": [[56, 0.5728181600570679], [60, 0.5717194080352783], [55, 0.5714824199676514], [32, 0.5647076368331909], [31, 0.5555441975593567], [46, 0.5554630756378174], [48, 0.5546152591705322], [45, 0.5530937910079956], [61, 0.5525554418563843], [54, 0.5518391132354736]], "An act or practice is unfair where it causes or is likely to cause substantial injury to consumers, the injury cannot be reasonably avoided by consumers, and the injury is not outweighed by countervailing benefits to consumers or to competition. In evaluating whether an injury is substantial, examiners generally look to monetary harm, though non-monetary harms may also be considered. An injury is not reasonably avoidable if the act or practice interferes with a consumer\u2019s ability to make informed decisions or take timely action to mitigate harm. Furthermore, the public policy underlying federal, state, or local laws may be considered with other evidence to determine whether an act or practice is unfair.": [[5, 0.8645153641700745], [9, 0.8440074920654297], [4, 0.8428182005882263], [3, 0.8276894092559814], [7, 0.8084925413131714], [8, 0.7999755144119263], [10, 0.7869733572006226], [2, 0.7811685800552368], [11, 0.7672353982925415], [6, 0.7312304973602295]], "Under the Dodd-Frank Act, an act or practice is abusive if it materially interferes with the ability of a consumer to understand a term or condition of a consumer financial product or service, or takes unreasonable advantage of a lack of understanding on the part of the consumer concerning the material risks, costs, or conditions of the product or service. Additionally, an abusive practice may take unreasonable advantage of the inability of the consumer to protect their own interests in selecting or using a consumer financial product or service, or take unreasonable advantage of the reasonable reliance by the consumer on a covered person to act in the interests of the consumer.": [[33, 0.8242987990379333], [34, 0.7997688055038452], [0, 0.7789623141288757], [2, 0.7700053453445435], [3, 0.7567768692970276], [41, 0.7346547842025757], [50, 0.7219974994659424], [8, 0.718930721282959], [39, 0.7128217220306396], [17, 0.7092560529708862]], "An entity engages in deceptive practices under the Consumer Financial Protection Act when it obscures material fee disclosures in fine print, footnotes, or inconspicuous locations within transaction documents. To avoid liability, fee information must be presented clearly and conspicuously, ensuring that consumers notice and comprehend the costs before committing to a product or service. Disclosures that are difficult to read, buried within dense legal text, or contradicted by prominent marketing representations fail to meet this standard. If a reasonable consumer would likely be misled regarding the total cost of the transaction due to the obscured placement of fees, the practice is considered deceptive and violates statutory prohibitions against unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts or practices.": [[21, 0.7621493339538574], [53, 0.7591314315795898], [19, 0.7560855150222778], [20, 0.753623366355896], [17, 0.7435446977615356], [24, 0.728156566619873], [40, 0.7273952960968018], [26, 0.726464569568634], [52, 0.7256972789764404], [29, 0.7206112146377563]], "Whether high-pressure sales tactics that urge customers to sign up quickly constitute an unfair, deceptive, or abusive act or practice depends upon a thorough analysis of the facts, legal standards, and the specific circumstances of the interaction. Creating an artificial sense of urgency or denying consumers adequate time to review critical terms can impair their ability to make an informed decision regarding a financial product or service. If such pressure prevents a reasonable consumer from understanding the material costs, risks, or conditions of the agreement, the practice may be deemed deceptive or abusive under the Dodd-Frank Act, particularly when combined with complex disclosures or obscured terms. Examiners must evaluate the overall net impression created by the timing, context, and presentation of the solicitation.": [[41, 0.7715926766395569], [2, 0.7690643668174744], [34, 0.756872296333313], [51, 0.7427324652671814], [50, 0.7394700646400452], [21, 0.7333848476409912], [52, 0.7329229712486267], [0, 0.7321385741233826], [42, 0.7309160232543945], [44, 0.7282450795173645]], "A representation, omission, or practice is misleading under the prohibition against deceptive acts or practices if it is likely to mislead a reasonable consumer and affect their decisions or conduct regarding a consumer financial product or service. In evaluating whether a disclosure is misleading, examiners must assess the overall net impression created by the communication. Even if literal statements are truthful, a representation may be misleading if prominent fine print, qualifying language, or disclaimers are presented in a manner that is insufficient to counteract the false or misleading general impression created by the primary claim. Furthermore, when evaluating claims directed at a targeted audience, such as elderly consumers or financially distressed individuals, reasonableness is analyzed from the perspective of that specific vulnerable group.": [[22, 0.8295425176620483], [18, 0.8147439956665039], [21, 0.8145331740379333], [19, 0.8106788396835327], [20, 0.7941364049911499], [40, 0.768556535243988], [28, 0.7669885158538818], [23, 0.7651140689849854], [52, 0.7555239200592041], [24, 0.7538123726844788]], "Federal consumer financial law and the examination procedures set forth by the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau do not establish a minimum credit score requirement for obtaining a mortgage loan. Neither the Dodd-Frank Act nor the regulations implementing the Truth in Lending Act or the Equal Credit Opportunity Act mandate specific credit score thresholds for mortgage origination. Instead, underwriting criteria, including minimum credit score requirements, are determined by individual financial institutions based on their internal risk appetites, secondary market guidelines, and applicable government-backed loan program requirements, such as those established by the Federal Housing Administration. Examiners must review whether an entity applies its credit score criteria consistently and fairly, ensuring that such policies do not result in unlawful discrimination or unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts or practices.": [[1, 0.704824686050415], [55, 0.6867375373840332], [40, 0.6839718818664551], [58, 0.6807142496109009], [46, 0.6706053614616394], [0, 0.6685089468955994], [34, 0.6681265830993652], [2, 0.6675431728363037], [41, 0.663219690322876], [32, 0.6624112725257874]]}